# 6. DATABASE CONSTRAINTS

Constraints are a mandatory part of the security and integrity model.

## 6.1 Primary keys

Every core entity must have a stable primary key.

## 6.2 Foreign keys

Use foreign keys for relationships that can be represented safely in the relational model.

Define deletion behaviour deliberately.

Examples:

- User profile deletion must not automatically erase required moderation evidence.
- Deleting a poll must not create orphaned vote records.
- Deleting a post must follow the approved comment and reaction retention strategy.
- Deleting a community must not silently corrupt membership records.

Do not default to CASCADE on every foreign key.

## 6.3 Unique constraints

Required logical uniqueness includes:

- Profile username.
- One reaction per user/post.
- One community membership per user/community.
- One notification preference per user/type.
- One answer per session/question where the game rules permit only one answer.
- One poll submission per user/poll under the approved voting model.
- One selected option per poll submission.

## 6.4 Check constraints

Examples:

- A user cannot block themselves.
- Poll expiration must be a valid timestamp.
- Display order cannot be negative.
- Scores cannot be negative unless an approved game rule requires otherwise.
- Multiple-choice selections must be validated against poll rules.
- A completed session must have a valid completion timestamp.
- Profile and content status values must be valid.

## 6.5 Database enforcement versus application enforcement

Use database constraints for invariant rules that can be expressed reliably in PostgreSQL.

Use trusted application logic for complex workflows and cross-entity business rules.

Use both where appropriate.

Frontend validation is never sufficient for data integrity.

