# 8. ROW LEVEL SECURITY (RLS) ARCHITECTURE

## 8.1 General requirements

RLS must be enabled on every exposed application table.

Policies must follow least privilege.

The database must not assume that a request is authorised merely because the user is authenticated.

## 8.2 Role model

The application supports:

- Guest.
- Registered user.
- Moderator.
- Administrator.
- Super administrator.

These are application permissions, not permission to trust client-provided role values.

Role resolution must come from a trusted server-controlled source.

## 8.3 Policy matrix

| Table | Guest | Registered user | Moderator | Administrator |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| profiles | Public fields only | Own profile and public profiles | Approved moderation access | Approved administrative access |
| user_preferences | No | Own | No default access | Restricted support access |
| user_consents | No | Own | No | Restricted compliance access |
| user_blocks | No | Own | No default access | Restricted |
| posts | Public published content | Public content and permitted writes | Moderation access | Administrative access |
| post_reactions | No private records | Approved reads and own writes | Approved moderation access | Administrative access |
| comments | Public permitted comments | Approved reads and own writes | Moderation access | Administrative access |
| polls | Public active polls | Approved reads and voting | Moderation access | Administrative access |
| poll_options | Public poll options | Approved reads | Moderation access | Administrative access |
| poll_votes | No | Own vote details and approved aggregate results | Restricted | Restricted |
| games | Public active definitions | Approved gameplay | No default access | Administrative access |
| game_questions | Public safe question content | Approved game access | No default access | Administrative access |
| game_options | Safe public options only | Approved gameplay | No default access | Administrative access |
| game_sessions | No | Own sessions | Restricted | Restricted |
| game_answers | No | Own sessions only | Restricted | Restricted |
| communities | Public communities | Approved membership operations | Approved access | Administrative access |
| community_members | No | Approved community membership data | Approved access | Administrative access |
| notifications | No | Own notifications | No | Restricted |
| notification_preferences | No | Own preferences | No | Restricted |
| reports | No | Own submitted reports where appropriate | Assigned or permitted reports | Approved access |
| moderation_actions | No | Only permitted user-facing status | Permitted moderation actions | Approved administrative access |
| moderation_audit_logs | No | No | Restricted audit access | Restricted audit access |
| locations | Approved public locations | Approved location access | No default access | Administrative access |
| user_location_preferences | No | Own preferences | No default access | Restricted |
| admin_audit_logs | No | No | No | Restricted privileged access |
| analytics_events | No | No direct access | No | Restricted reporting access |

This matrix describes intended access, not a ready-to-copy RLS implementation.

Every row requires a specific SQL policy and test.

## 8.4 RLS policy design

Policies must define:

- SELECT.
- INSERT.
- UPDATE.
- DELETE.

Where a user may update their own row, `USING` and `WITH CHECK` must both prevent changes to protected ownership or permission fields.

Never use a policy that permits all authenticated users to modify all records.

## 8.5 Profile security

A public profile view should expose only approved public fields.

Do not rely on hiding private columns in frontend components.

Use an appropriately designed database view or restricted API response.

If a view is used, verify its security behaviour, ownership context and RLS interaction. Do not assume a view automatically inherits the intended access restrictions.

## 8.6 Anonymous content security

Anonymous posts must still enforce:

- Authenticated ownership.
- Reporting.
- Blocking.
- Moderation access.
- Abuse prevention.

The database must retain the internal author relationship while public retrieval omits identifying information.

## 8.7 Vote security

Users must not be able to:

- Vote as another user.
- Change vote ownership.
- Insert duplicate submissions.
- Select an option belonging to another poll.
- Modify authoritative result counts.
- Update closed polls.
- Access other users' private vote records.

Vote operations must use trusted transaction-safe operations.

## 8.8 Game security

Users must not be able to:

- Assign their own score.
- Mark answers correct.
- Change session ownership.
- Submit answers to unrelated sessions.
- Reuse completed sessions.
- Retrieve protected answer keys through public queries.

## 8.9 Administrative security

Moderation and administrative tables require strict access policies.

The application must not permit users to modify their own role or insert administrative audit records.

Privileged operations must execute through trusted server-side paths.

